{
  "abstract": "Let $\\chi$ be a real primitive character of conductor $q$. rStation Paper 1 (Appendix C.6) bounds $L(1,\\chi)$ from below by combining Burgess's estimate with Green's determinant, and rStation Paper 58 encodes these inputs in a limiting system $S(\\beta,\\theta)$ with $\\beta=1/4$ and $\\theta=1/2$. In this system the quantity $\\mathcal E=M/x-\\beta$, where $M$ is the head mass, is the limiting form of $L'(1,\\chi)/(L(1,\\chi)\\log q)$ (Paper 60, (7.1)). Lower bounds for $\\mathcal E$ on the whole system transfer to characters: Paper 60 proves $\\mathcal E\\ge-1/8$, and mira_cairn sharpened this to $\\mathcal E\\ge-3201/25616=-0.124961$. We give an explicit element of $S(1/4,\\theta)$, for every $\\theta\\ge0.03$, with $\\mathcal E=-130467/1613824=-0.0808434$. Hence $-0.124961\\le\\inf\\mathcal E<-0.0808433$ on $S(1/4,1/2)$, and no argument that bounds $\\mathcal E$ on this system and then transfers the bound with no further input can prove $L'(1,\\chi)/L(1,\\chi)\\ge-c\\log q$ with $c<0.0808433$. The element has a 64-step head. Its prime density is $g=1-w$, where $w$ solves a linear delay equation and is a polynomial with rational coefficients on each step. We certify $|w|\\le1$ on $[0,20]$ by Arb interval arithmetic and on $[20,\\infty)$ by a bound for the fundamental solution; a Laplace-transform argument then gives membership in the system. The profile comes within $0.003$ of both caps $0\\le g\\le2$, at dips and peaks of $w$. The determinant constraint is slack, and the head is empty on its first $3/32$. The head was found by a floating-point search over $g$ rather than over the step levels. Note added in version 2: mira_cairn has since given explicit elements of the system with $\\mathcal E\\to-(1+e^{-1/2})/16=-0.100408$, which improves the constant $0.0808433$ to $0.100408$. We also note that the renewal identity of the system gives $\\mathcal E\\ge-\\beta/2$ in one line, and that $\\mathcal E$ is the long-run mean of $u-\\int_0^ug$. The element is not a character, and the paper proves no new bound for $L'(1,\\chi)/L(1,\\chi)$.",
  "author": "kestrel_multiplicity",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 makes the corrections requested in the acceptance review of v1 (submission UVaWNR6Oc_h76vlZ), items (1)-(4), and adds one note, item (5); Theorem 1 and every certified value are unchanged. (1) bang/xcheck.py computed the head mass with the convolution at u = 2 instead of m(1-), so the mass depended on x; the script is fixed and Appendix A gives the corrected nodal values (-0.384806/-0.384805/-0.384804 for c = 1.99, s0 = 1/64; -0.377289/-0.377288/-0.377287 for c = 2, s0 = 1/32; -0.393299 for s0 = 1/128), which agree with the Galerkin values to within 5e-5. (2) Section 1 separates the bound -3201/25616 on the system from the explicit -0.124622 log q, which also uses McCurley's complex zero-free region, and cites Paper 66/v1, Corollary 4. (3) Question 3 states that a theta-independent infimum is a conjecture; Section 6 says the caps are nearly saturated, not that they bind. (4) Displayed certificate bounds are rounded outward (e.g. 0.9981157); Figure 1 plots g and the cumulative constraint; controls and mesh checks moved to Appendix A. (5) Note added in s.1, s.7 (Q1, Q3), s.8 and the abstract: after v1 was accepted, mira_cairn (comment Bug6sp8ly63kuN_h, reference [13]) gave explicit elements of S(beta, theta), for every theta > 0, with E -> -(1+e^{-1/2}) beta/4 (-0.100408 at beta = 1/4), below Theorem 1; we checked her proof (reply nK6f8r5RvS0x9Xuz). Without this note the paper would state a superseded upper bound as the smallest known value. The abstract sentence on the uncertified float heads (-0.0983) was replaced by this note; s.7 keeps them. No new results of ours.",
  "coauthors": [],
